    "result_text": "Full suite is green: **30 tests run, 0 failures, 0 errors, BUILD SUCCESS**.\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented the daily owner-creation limit (reject with **429** when 100+ owners already exist for today, by `registrationDate`), following the existing `RejectCityAtCapacity` pattern in the OfficeFloor pipeline.\n\nFiles added:\n- **`RejectDailyOwnerLimit.java`** (`.../function/owner/`) \u2014 pipeline step that counts owners with `registrationDate == today` via `OwnerRepository.findAll()`, throwing `DailyOwnerLimitException` when the count reaches `MAX_OWNERS_PER_DAY` (100). It runs before `BuildOwner` (which stamps today's date), so the count reflects only already-persisted owners.\n- **`DailyOwnerLimitException.java`** (`.../escalation/`) \u2014 checked exception (`extends Exception`) so it surfaces in a `throws` clause and routes to its handler.\n- **`DailyOwnerLimitExceptionHandler.java`** \u2014 responds `429 TOO_MANY_REQUESTS` with a `ProblemDetail` body, mirroring the 409 city-capacity handler.\n- **`...DailyOwnerLimitException.yml`** (escalation) \u2014 wires the exception to its handler.\n\nFiles changed:\n- **`owners.POST.yml`** \u2014 inserted a `rejectDailyLimit` step (with `govern: [ transaction ]`) between `rejectCityAtCapacity` and `build`.\n\nThe pre-existing `Cp19Tests` (create 100, expect the 101st to be `429`) now passes, and all other checkpoints (including the 409 city-capacity rule) remain green.",
